%I A399847 #22 Sep 24 2026 15:42:36
%S A399847 107,607,2203,4253,11213,86243
%N A399847 Mersenne prime exponents k such that 2^k-2 is not a practical number.
%C A399847 No even exponent k other than k = 2 can be a practical number of the form 2^k-1 (see comments for A399756).
%C A399847 The first Mersenne prime exponent k such that 2^k-2 is not practical is 107 and all Mersenne primes prior to 2^107-1 are sandwich between twin practical numbers 2^k-2 and 2^k because powers of 2 are always practical.
%C A399847 756839, 1398269, 24036583 and 37156667 are also terms. For these terms, the sum of the divisors of their lower set of prime factors < 10^4 fails the Stewart/Sierpiński characterization for practicality.
%H A399847 Factordb, <a href="https://factordb.com">Factorizing Data Base</a>.
%H A399847 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cunningham_Project">Cunningham Project</a>.
%H A399847 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclotomic_polynomial">Cyclotomic polynomial</a>.
%H A399847 Wolfram, <a href="https://demonstrations.wolfram.com/FactorizingMersenneNumbers">Factorizing Mersenne Numbers</a>.
%e A399847 a(2)=607, because it is the second occurrence of a Mersenne prime exponent k such that 2^k-2 is not practical. The prime factorization of the lowest primes for 2^607-2 is 2*3^2*7*607*C where C is a composite coprime with 2, 3, 7 and 607. It can easily be seen that it cannot be practical because the sum of the divisors of the lower set of prime factors 2*3^2*7 is 312 and the next prime factor 607 > 312+1 fails the Stewart/Sierpiński characterization for practicality.
%Y A399847 Cf. A000043, A005153, A064078, A267124, A399756.
